\section{Replicate weight matrix}{

The replicate weight matrix is \strong{not stored} in the object. Only the
column names are stored in \verb{@variables$repweights}. Variance estimation
computes the matrix on demand:
\code{as.matrix(design@data[, design@variables$repweights])}.
}

\section{Memory usage}{

Each call to an estimation function (e.g., \code{\link[=get_means]{get_means()}}, \code{\link[=get_totals]{get_totals()}})
materialises the full replicate weight matrix from the data frame. For large
designs (e.g., ACS PUMS with 500k+ rows × 80 replicates), this is roughly
\code{nrow * n_replicates * 8} bytes per call (~363 MB for ACS Wyoming × 80).
If you are estimating many variables, this is repeated for each call.
This behaviour matches the \code{survey} package reference implementation.
}

